SU(2) log-concave-square-root global-payment family stress

COMMAND
make -f Makefile.research probe_su2_aim_square_root_families
./probe_su2_aim_square_root_families 120 60

OUTPUT
SU2_AIM_SQUARE_ROOT_FAMILIES maximum_length=120 maximum_level=120 threads=36 roots=5966 suffixes=1185555 reserve_admissible_suffixes=1185555 payment_checks=332416738 payment_failures=0
FIRST_PAYMENT_FAILURE level=-1 shell=-1 factor=-1 radius=-1 value=0 boundary=0 root=[] square=[] reserve=[]

METHOD
For every support length through 120, the audit constructs exact
log-concave roots from the following deliberately different boundary
regimes:

  constant intervals;
  rising and falling linear ramps;
  symmetric tents and trapezoids;
  binomial rows;
  increasing and decreasing powers of 2 and 3.

It also shifts each family through the first three admissible leading
zeros.  Duplicate roots are removed, leaving 5,966 exact roots.  For
each root p it forms the ordinary SU(2) character square m=p^2.  At
every half-level through 60 it constructs every nonempty proper
affine-image suffix D_n and its reserve u_n=BD_n.

All 1,185,555 suffixes have coefficientwise nonnegative reserve.  For
each reserve-admissible suffix, every reduced proposed factor q and
every row r is checked against the exact payment

  rho_(n;q,r)(m)
    - sum_s max(-(T_q)_(r,s),0)u_(n,s)(m).

All 332,416,738 exact payments are nonnegative.  This is a structured
bounded stress of the log-concave-square-root target AIMSRP, not its
proof.

The thread count is chosen from hardware concurrency, current machine
load, and available RAM, using a conservative 64 MiB allowance per
worker.  The exact roots and counts are independent of scheduling.

COMPILER FLAGS
g++ -O2 -std=c++20 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Wconversion \
  -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror -pthread \
  probe_su2_aim_square_root_families.cpp \
  -o probe_su2_aim_square_root_families

